  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nTonight through Saturday Night:\n\nExpect primarily quiet conditions through the day on Saturday.\nTonight will be the last relatively cool night for a while as we\nenter the advertised much warmer and more humid pattern into\nnext week. On Saturday, decaying convection will cross the mid \nand upper MS Valley, which may yield some sprinkles or light \nshowers to parts of northern Illinois during the afternoon,\nparticularly closer to the IL/WI state line where we have some\nlow PoPs. The main sensible weather effect will be fairly thick\nmid-level overcast, which will likely keep temperatures a bit \ncooler. The mostly cloudy skies paired with dew points in the\nlower to mid 60s will make for an all in all comfortable late\nAugust day. \n\nOn Saturday night, an initial warm front will lift north into\nWisconsin. Conceptually, the best chance for nocturnal \nconvection will be closer to the front (ie. somewhere over\ncentral/southern Wisconsin). This will be the focus for stronger\nwarm and moist advection and a better chance to erode MUCIN in\nan overall modest mid-level lapse rate regime. Can't rule out\nsome spotty showers late Saturday night into early Sunday over\nparts of far northern Illinois, though forecast soundings look\nprohibitive enough for lightning generation to forego any\nmention of thunder in the grids. It will be a milder night than\nthe past several amidst steady south-southeast winds.\n\nSunday Onward:\n\nStrong mid-level ridging will amplify northeastward from the\nsouthern Plains and center over the Ohio Valley next week. While\nwe're past peak crop evapotranspiration, it will be a very warm\nto hot and generally uncomfortably humid week. Sunday will\nfeature well above normal highs solidly in the mid to upper 80s\nalong with breezy southwesterly winds in the wake of a secondary,\nstronger warm frontal passage. Monday then has a decent chance \nto be the first 90F reading of August at Chicago and Rockford, \non the last day of the month! September and thus meteorological\nfall (already?!) will get off to a summer-like start with \nadditional 90+ temps and daytime heat indices flirting with 100F\nin spots possible on Tuesday and Wednesday. If the ridge \nflattens out sufficiently later in the week, weak surface high \npressure may enable a backdoor cold front to sag south and bring\nsome cooling, particularly closer to the lake. \n\nThe mid and upper level ridging currently looks amplified \nenough to result in more pronounced capping and a better chance \nfor \"ring of fire\" type convective activity to remain primarily \nnorth of the area until perhaps later in the week, when the \nridge may flatten out some, as alluded to above. Northern \nIllinois (especially with northward extent) is close enough to \nthe precarious northern periphery of the ridge to warrant \noccasional lower end (20-30%) thunderstorm chances (focused at \nnight) through midweek. Ensemble guidance variance, even later \nin the week, is such that periodic PoPs are still only in the \n20-30% range.\n\nCastro",
